messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nSmithtown University' s fund-raisers succeeded in getting donations from 80 percent of the potential donors they contacted. This success rate, exceptionally high for university fund-raisers, does not indicate that they were doing a good job. On the contrary, since the people most likely to donate are those who have donated in the past, good fund-raisers constantly try less-likely prospects in an effort to expand the donor base. The high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, provides more support for the argument?\n# Options:\nA) This year the average size of the donations to smithtown university from new donors when the university's fund-raisers had contacted was larger than the average size of donations from donors who had given to the university before.\nB) The majority of the donations that fund-raisers succeeded in getting for smithtown university this year were from donors who had never given to the university before.\nC) This year most of the donations that came to smithtown university from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university's fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\nD) Smithtown university's fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.. I think the correct option is: C\n", "To solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option best supports the argument that the high success rate of Smithtown University's fund-raisers does not indicate good performance but rather insufficient canvassing effort.\n\nLet's analyze each option:.\n\nA) This year the average size of the donations to Smithtown University from new donors when the university's fund-raisers had contacted was larger than the average size of donations from donors who had given to the university before.\n- This option talks about the size of donations from new versus old donors. It does not directly address the issue of canvassing effort or the success rate being due to contacting only likely donors.\n\nB) The majority of the donations that fund-raisers succeeded in getting for Smithtown University this year were from donors who had never given to the university before.\n- This option suggests that the majority of successful donations came from new donors. However, the argument states that good fund-raisers should be contacting less-likely prospects to expand the donor base, not that the majority of donations should come from new donors. This does not directly support the argument about insufficient canvassing effort.\n\nC) This year most of the donations that came to Smithtown University from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university's fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\n- This option indicates that many donations from previous donors were made without any contact from the fund-raisers. This supports the argument that the fund-raisers are not doing enough canvassing, as they are not even contacting the most likely donors (previous donors), and these donors are still donating.\n\nD) Smithtown University's fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.\n- This option compares the success rate of contacting new donors with other universities. It does not directly support the argument about insufficient canvassing effort, as it does not address the issue of contacting only likely donors. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
