messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nWe ought to pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art. Its other, extrinsic properties are irrelevant to our aesthetic interactions with it. For example, when we look at a painting we should consider only what is directly presented in our experience of it. What is really aesthetically relevant, ttherefore, is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is added to the premises?\n# Options:\nA) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.\nB) Only an artwork's intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.\nC) It is possible in theory for an artwork to symbolize nothing.\nD) What an artwork symbolizes involves only extrinsic properties of that work.. I think the correct option is: A\n", 'To solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option, when added to the premises, would make the conclusion logically follow. The conclusion we are aiming to support is that what is aesthetically relevant is what a work of art directly presents to experience, not what it symbolizes.\n\nLet\'s analyze each option:.\n\nA) "There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.".\n- This statement is about distinguishing symbolic properties but does not directly link to the conclusion that only intrinsic properties are aesthetically relevant.\n\nB) "Only an artwork\'s intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.".\n- This directly supports the conclusion that what is aesthetically relevant is what the artwork directly presents to experience, aligning with the idea that intrinsic properties are the only ones that matter.\n\nC) "It is possible in theory for an artwork to symbolize nothing.".\n- This statement is about the possibility of an artwork not symbolizing anything, which does not directly support the conclusion about the relevance of intrinsic properties.\n\nD) "What an artwork symbolizes involves only extrinsic properties of that work.".\n- This statement supports the idea that symbolic properties are extrinsic, which indirectly supports the conclusion by reinforcing that extrinsic properties are irrelevant to aesthetic interactions. However, it does not directly state that intrinsic properties are the only relevant ones. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
