messages:   88%|########7 | 525/600 [04:07<01:10,  1.06it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nWittgenstein was a student of Moore, a famous philosopher at Cambridge University. One day, philosopher Russell asked Moore, who is your best student? Moore said without hesitation: Wittgenstein. why? because among all the students, only he always showed a blank look when listening to the class, and there were always endless questions. later, Wittgenstein is more famous than Russell. Someone asked, why did Russell fall behind? Wittgenstein said: he has no problem.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, this paragraph is intended to illustrate that:\n# Options:\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success\nB) Curiosity is the internal driving force of research\nC) Appreciation is an important factor in success\nD) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress. I think the correct option is: B\n', 'To solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the context and analyze each option in relation to the information provided.\n\n1. **Context Analysis:**.\n   - Wittgenstein was a student of Moore.\n   - Moore considered Wittgenstein his best student because Wittgenstein always had questions.\n   - Wittgenstein became more famous than Russell.\n   - Wittgenstein attributed Russell\'s falling behind to the fact that Russell had no problem (or no questions/problems to solve).\n\n2. **Option Analysis:**.\n   - **A) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success:**.\n     - The context does highlight that Wittgenstein\'s questioning nature was a significant factor in his success. However, the word "always" is too absolute and not necessarily supported by the context.\n   - **B) Curiosity is the internal driving force of research:**.\n     - This option aligns well with the context, as Wittgenstein\'s curiosity (his endless questions) is highlighted as a key factor in his success.\n   - **C) Appreciation is an important factor in success:**.\n     - The context does not mention appreciation as a factor in success. It focuses more on questioning and problem-solving.\n   - **D) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress:**.\n     - This option also aligns well with the context. Wittgenstein\'s success is attributed to his ability to find problems (questions), while Russell\'s lack of problems is seen as a reason for his relative lack of progress.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**.\n   - Both options B and D are strong contenders as they both relate to the importance of questioning and problem-solving. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
